### MCP-READONLY-1

- `generate_mcp_cli_tools.py`: fix the token-normalization bug in `classify_command` — path tokens are normalized `-`→`_` but `DESTRUCTIVE_TOKENS`/`SAFE_LEAF_TOKENS` stored hyphenated spellings, so every hyphenated set entry was dead (`trust-score`, `network-stats`, `bridge-status`, `import-wallet`, `top-up`, …). Both sets now match normalized copies.
- `SAFE_LEAF_TOKENS` gained 17 verified-safe leaf tokens (`active`, `agents`, `allocations`, `circuits`, `estimate-fee`, `island-info`, `list-deposits`, `list-gpus`, `list-islands`, `orderbook`, `orders`, `pending`, `predict`, `price`, `query`, `results`, `scan`); `FORCE_READ_ONLY_PATHS` gained `market escrow status`, `dashboard customer`, `dashboard shop`; `FORCE_DESTRUCTIVE_PATHS` gained `ipfs island swarm-key` (signs a wallet challenge and can write swarm.key — the normalization fix would otherwise have promoted it).
- Net effect: 33 generated `aitbc_*` tools move from destructive-gated to read-only — the verified read leaves from the SM-B2 replay (`transactions pending|estimate-fee`, `exchange-island orderbook|orders`, `ai results`, `gpu list-gpus`, `gpu-onchain query|allocations`, `agent discover agents`, `reputation trust-score`, `analytics predict`, `security scan`, `crosschain bridge-status`, `bridge pending|security-status`, `explorer network-stats|block-by-hash|blocks-by-address|non-empty-blocks|top-addresses|transaction-by-hash`, `zk circuits`, `dispute active`, `market escrow status`, `market exchange price|list-deposits`, `dashboard customer|shop`, `node island island-info|list-islands`, `monitor campaign-stats`, `edge gpu list-gpus`, `simulate price`). `ai service test`, `monitor alerts`, `ai refund-sweep` and `ipfs island swarm-key` stay gated on purpose.
- Curated tools: `download_ipfs`/`download_market_ipfs` relabelled destructive with the dry_run/confirm gate (both write a file on the remote host — `read_only_hint` was wrong); `list_node_islands` repointed from the dead `island-list-islands` subcommand to the real `node island list-islands` leaf; `list_aitbc_ipfs_rentals` dropped (`ipfs rentals` does not exist in the CLI tree).


### MCP-SECRETS-1

- `generate_mcp_cli_tools.py`: secret-named options are now skipped by pattern (`SECRET_VALUE_RE`), not by exact name — `wallet_password`, `admin_private_key`, `*_private_key`, `api_key`, `secret`, `access_secret` and friends can no longer surface as MCP tool parameters. `*_file`/`*_path`/`*_dir` names stay exempt (the accepted file form), `SECRET_OPTION_ALLOWLIST` is the explicit exception list, and a positional secret argument would be skipped the same way.
- 21 generated `aitbc_*` tools lose secret-value params: `auth login` drops `private_key` (keeps `private_key_file`); six bridge tools plus `bridge lock|unlock` drop raw key/`wallet_password` params; `network force-sync` drops `admin_private_key`; `exchange register` drops `api_key`+`secret_key`; `http call` and `ipfs island swarm-key` drop `api_key` (both resolve the configured key server-side); `market download`/`ipfs download` drop `access_secret`; four `trade` tools drop `api_key` (config/`BLOCKCHAIN_RPC_API_KEY`), `trade settle` also drops the required HTLC `secret`.
- Curated tools: `download_market_ipfs` drops `access_secret`; `get_ipfs_rental_token` moves to `access_secret_file` and `register_ipfs_rental_token` to `token_file` — both read the secret-holding file on the MCP host and scrub the value from serialized output (`_scrub_secrets`) so the echoed command cannot leak it back into the transcript.
- Leaves whose CLI has no file/env form lose real-run capability until the follow-up CLI options land: `--private-key-file` on `bridge attest|confirm|ingest-header|register-validator|sign-proof|store-header` and `network force-sync`, `--password-file` on `bridge lock|unlock`, `--access-secret-file` on `market|ipfs download`, `--secret-file` on `trade settle`, env/file credential input on `exchange register`.
- New schema test scans every mcp-server module (generated + curated) and fails on any parameter matching the secret regex unless allow-listed with a reason.


### MCP-RESULT-1 — MCP results carry the failure signal; timeout output decoded; read_aitbc_cli path gate closed (SM-07, SM-09, SM-05; #420)

- Every command-result envelope now carries `ok` (bool) and `exit_code`:
  `ok` is `returncode == 0` *and* no error pattern in the first 15 lines of
  stdout/stderr, closing the SP-13 class where exit-0 CLI errors surfaced as
  MCP success with the error only in the body.  When the pattern — not the
  return code — makes `ok` false, `error_hint` names the offending line.
  `{"error": …}` envelopes stamp `ok:false` and dry-run previews `ok:true`
  via `_json` so refusal/gating results are distinguishable too.
- Timeouts keep `returncode:-1` and gain `timed_out:true`; partial stdout/
  stderr are now decoded (`errors="replace"`) instead of byte-mangled
  `b'\xe2…'` reprs — `TimeoutExpired.stdout` stays bytes even under
  `text=True`.
- `read_aitbc_cli` token-splitting hole closed: the destructive-path check
  now runs over the *effective* path `group + subcommand + args` (Click
  consumes leading positionals as path tokens) with prefix matching on
  `DESTRUCTIVE_AITBC_PATHS`, so `["alerts","list"]`, `["alerts"]`+args,
  `subcommand=None`+args, and string spellings classify identically.
  Previously `read_aitbc_cli(group="monitor", subcommand=["alerts","list"])`
  executed while `["alerts"]`+args was refused — and `args` alone could
  supply any destructive leaf (e.g. `monitor alerts remove`).
- Tests: error-pattern table (20 false / 10 true cases), timeout byte
  decode, both spellings for every multi-token destructive path.



### MCP-MISC-1 — leaf --format forwarded on typed tools, canonical --bridge-id emitted, scratch env/cwd on the generic CLI lane (SM-08, SM-10, SM-11; #421)

- Generated typed tools now forward leaf-declared output-format options:
  ``format``/``output``/``output_format`` were removed from the generator's
  skip list, so ~90 tools gained a ``format``/``output_format`` parameter
  (Literal-typed from the leaf's Choice). ``OUTPUT_FORMAT_OPTION``'s twin
  ``--output``/``--format`` Click options sharing one param name are deduped
  to a single parameter, ``--format`` preferred. The global ``--output=json``
  still precedes the group; a leaf option wins where the leaf honours it, and
  a non-JSON response simply yields no parsed ``json`` field in the envelope.
- ``get_flag_name`` now prefers the first-declared long option instead of the
  longest: ``crosschain bridge-status`` emits ``--bridge-id`` (the canonical
  spelling the CLI epilog and scenarios use) rather than the ``--transfer-id``
  alias. It was the only multi-long-option leaf in the tree.
- ``run_aitbc_cli`` and ``read_aitbc_cli`` accept ``env``/``cwd`` for scratch
  plays: env names are allow-listed (``HOME`` or non-secret ``AITBC_*``
  names), every value must be a path under ``/var/tmp/``, and ``cwd`` must be
  under ``/var/tmp/``. Values travel as a POSIX ``cd <dir> && KEY=value``
  prefix in the remote command string (SSH SendEnv is not used); the
  mutating-command gate is unchanged.
- Bug fixed on the local exec path: ``_run_remote`` previously ran
  ``shlex.split(command)`` directly, so a baked ``KEY=value`` prefix or
  ``cd <dir> &&`` clause became argv[0] and any env-using tool failed with
  FileNotFoundError on the MCP host's own node. The local branch now peels
  POSIX prefixes off the string and applies them as real child env/cwd.


### MCP-ECHO-1 — secret-bearing argument values masked in every echoed command (SM-03; #422)

- New ``mcp-server/aitbc_mcp_mask.py``: ``mask_command`` (string or argv
  list) and ``mask_envelope``. ``_json`` — the one serialisation step every
  tool module returns through — now serialises a masked copy, so dry-run,
  confirmation, result, timeout and error envelopes from the generic lanes,
  the HTTP/RPC tools and all generated tools are covered without touching
  the generator. The argv handed to ``subprocess`` is unchanged.
- Masked, with the option name kept and the value replaced by
  ``<redacted>``: values of secret-named options (``--api-key=v`` and
  ``--password v``), ``NAME=value`` env prefixes and positionals, query and
  ``--params`` pairs, URL userinfo passwords, ``Authorization``/``X-*-Key``
  header values, ``Bearer`` tokens and secret-named keys inside JSON bodies.
  Names use the same deny list as ``aitbc_cli.commands.system``'s
  ``_field_name_denied``, copied into the mask so the MCP server does not
  need ``aitbc_cli`` on its path to start; a parity test in
  ``tests/test_aitbc_mcp_echo_mask.py`` is the only import of the CLI
  original and fails when the copy drifts (connection-string names exempt;
  ``access_key``/public keys and ``*_file``/``*_path``/``*_dir``/``*-stdin``
  names stay visible). A truncated JSON body under an unbalanced quote is
  masked by a loose-pair rule in the regex fallback.
- Stays visible: tx/block hashes (a bare 64-hex is only masked when a
  secret-named option sits next to it), addresses, CIDs, wallet/name values,
  ids. An echo without secrets is returned unchanged.
- ``_scrub_secrets`` stays as is: it replaces known values in the whole
  output (stdout included); the mask is pattern-based on echo fields only.
- Tests: every pattern, the must-not-mask list, list/string forms, error
  text, byte-identical executed argv (local and ssh), and an AST coverage
  test that fails when an envelope is serialised without the mask.
